            <ArticleTitle>Increase of body mass index (BMI) from 1.5 to 3 years of age augments the degree of insulin resistance corresponding to BMI at 12 years of age.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>455-457</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/jpem-2016-0227</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/jpem.2017.30.issue-4/jpem-2016-0227/jpem-2016-0227.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To elucidate the effect of early growth patterns on the metabolic sensitivity to adiposity, we examined the relationship between the homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR) and body mass index (BMI) levels at 12 years of age in 101 boys and 91 girls in a birth cohort. Children with an increase in BMI from the ages of 1.5 to 3 years exhibited a greater increase of HOMA-IR per BMI increase at 12 years of age compared to those with a decrease in BMI or stable BMI from 1.5 to 3 years. This suggests that children who show an increase in BMI from 1.5 to 3 years, a period normally characterized by a decreased or stable BMI, are more prone to developing insulin resistance at 12 years of age.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
